+      "Vermilia": "The separate Parasite entry says living targets save vs. poison to avoid infection. Undead contract it automatically, then can save vs. death magic each day at day’s end to avoid that day’s damage. That save carries a penalty equal to the day’s vermilia damage. A Legacy user takes an additional −1 per Legacy on vermilia saves. Damage doubles daily from 1 HP on day one until cure disease, heal, or the setting’s special remedy treats it. It counts as a +2 magical weapon for purposes of affecting undead or other magical creatures. The text does not explicitly specify how the bite’s 20% check interacts with the general save. See printed pp. 85–86.",
+      "Parasites": "Cardinal ticks can infest this creature. If the specimen is infested, a melee encounter leaves the combatants with 5% infestation. Infestation increases by 15 percentage points each time its host attempts to use a Legacy, up to 50% in nonhumanoids. A cinnabryl ointment clears it. Scratching costs 1 HP per percentage point removed unless the special Jibarú medicine is used. See Parasite, printed p. 85.",
+      "Red Curse": "Cinnavixens never gain Legacies and do not need cinnabryl. Wild ones can find local cinnabryl by scent. Tamed specimens lose this ability.",
+      "Encounter ecology": "Uncommon temperate forest and mountain carnivore, active at dusk and dawn. Family organization and 1d4 appearing. It usually flees unless feeding or protecting pups, bites, then springs away. Mates stay together for life and typically raise 4–6 pups in a burrow. It hunts rodents, chiefly voats, and often shelters near rock ledges or roots. An undamaged treated pelt is worth up to 5 gp. Inheritors protect the animals because they can find cinnabryl."
