+    "appearance": "A gray-black wild-boar lookalike with short woolly hair, a bristled mane along its spine, rust-red eyes, and curved lower tusks up to 12 in. long. It stands about 3 ft. at the shoulder.",
+    "habitats": ["Any land", "Caves", "Robrenn forests", "Cursed Savage Coast"],
+    "sections": {
+      "Printed source": "Savage Coast Monstrous Compendium Appendix, pp. 103–104. ",
+      "Attacks": "One attack per round for 3d4 damage. It charges a bearer of red steel or cinnabryl, often with Spikes active, and ignores the trick of distracting it with a few dropped red steel spikes.",
+      "Eating metal": "Detects cinnabryl, red steel and steel seed by scent from up to 90 ft. Make a successful attack roll to touch an item made of these metals. On contact it immediately crumbles into edible powder. A weapon of one of those metals that hits the succulus makes contact and is subject to the same disintegration procedure. A magical item with a listed weapon or armor bonus has a 10% chance per +1 to resist. Other magical items made from the listed metals count as +2 (20%). Feeding takes one round regardless of the meal size.",
+      "Dig and Spikes Legacies": "Both are always present. Each is usable three times a day. Digging removes a 5-ft. cube of earth, sand or mud within 10 ft. in one round. A creature within 1 ft. of the pit must pass a Dexterity check or fall inside. A pit in loose earth or mud soon collapses. The appendix’s monster Spikes rules last one round per HD (five rounds). A creature touching the caster takes 2d4 damage. Add 1d6 to a natural physical attack other than a bite. A cinnabryl-rich diet usually protects the succulus from Affliction.",
+      "Encounters and ecology": "Rare family group of 1d12, active by day. Most are encountered with wild swine. Each encounter has a 5% chance of one half-strength offspring, with no separate juvenile stat block in the book. Two succuli produce a succulus. Offspring of a succulus and wild swine have a 5% chance to be a succulus. Eats ore but prefers refined metal. The metal-laden flesh is inedible. Cured hide can make armor equivalent to studded leather (AC 7). A trained young succulus can locate cinnabryl deposits.",
+      "Source notes": "The creature entry calls the two Legacies Dig and Spikes, and uses singular Spike in the combat prose. Digging is the Campaign Book name. This profile uses the appendix’s monster form of Spikes."
+    }
